.TH UWG-QUICK 8 "2026-11-21" "UmbraWG" "System Administration"

.SH NAME
uwg-quick \- set up a UmbraWG interface from a configuration file

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uwg-quick
[ \fBup\fP | \fBdown\fP | \fBsave\fP | \fBstrip\fP ] [ \fICONFIG_FILE\fP | \fIINTERFACE\fP ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uwg-quick
is a wrapper around
.BR uwg (8)
that creates an interface, assigns addresses, adds routes, configures DNS and
firewall rules, and executes hooks. It is a Linux port of the classic
\fBwg-quick\fP script.

\fICONFIG_FILE\fP is the path to a configuration file. If only
\fIINTERFACE\fP is given, the configuration is read from
.IR /etc/umbrawg/INTERFACE.conf .
The file uses the format documented in
.BR uwg (8),
plus the extra \fB[Interface]\fP keys listed below.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBup\fP \fI<interface>\fP
Creates and configures the interface. If the kernel module is not loaded and
a userspace implementation is available, the daemon named by
.B UWG_QUICK_USERSPACE_IMPLEMENTATION
(default \fBumbrawg\fP) is started with the interface name as its argument.
.TP
\fBdown\fP \fI<interface>\fP
Removes firewall rules and routes, runs \fBPreDown\fP and \fBPostDown\fP, and
deletes the interface. If \fBSaveConfig\fP is true, the current configuration
is written back to the configuration file first.
.TP
\fBsave\fP \fI<interface>\fP
Writes the current configuration of the interface to its configuration file.
.TP
\fBstrip\fP \fI<interface>\fP
Prints the configuration with \fBuwg-quick\fP-only keys removed, suitable for
.B uwg setconf
or for comparing configurations.

.SH CONFIGURATION
In addition to the keys accepted by
.BR uwg (8),
the \fB[Interface]\fP section accepts:
.TP
\fBAddress\fP
Address with optional CIDR mask; may be specified multiple times or as a
comma-separated list.
.TP
\fBDNS\fP
DNS server or search domain. DNS is configured with
.BR resolvconf (8)
when available.
.TP
\fBMTU\fP
Explicit MTU. If omitted, the MTU is derived from the route to the first
peer endpoint or from the system default route, minus the tunnel overhead.
While FEC is enabled the MTU derived by \fBuwg setconf\fP is preserved: the
automatic or explicit value never raises it. With \fBUnderlayMTU = 1500\fP
this keeps 1368 bytes on the kernel backend and 1352 bytes on the userspace
backend.
.TP
\fBTable\fP
Routing table for added routes. \fBauto\fP (default) uses the main table and
policy routing for default routes; \fBoff\fP disables route creation; any
number or name selects a table explicitly.
.TP
\fBPreUp\fP, \fBPostUp\fP, \fBPreDown\fP, \fBPostDown\fP
Shell snippets executed by
.BR bash (1)
at the corresponding phases. The string \fB%i\fP is replaced with the
interface name. Each key may appear multiple times and runs in order.
.TP
\fBSaveConfig\fP
If \fBtrue\fP, the interface configuration is saved on \fBdown\fP.
.TP
\fBFEC\fP, \fBUnderlayMTU\fP
Accepted from
.BR uwg (8)
and applied to both the kernel and the userspace backend. They select the FEC
mode and the MTU of the path between the peers respectively.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B UWG_QUICK_USERSPACE_IMPLEMENTATION
Userspace daemon used when the kernel module is unavailable. Default:
\fBumbrawg\fP.
.TP
.B WG_COLOR_MODE
Passed through to
.BR uwg (8).
.TP
.B WG_HIDE_KEYS
Passed through to
.BR uwg (8).
.PP
The deprecated \fBUWG_COLOR_MODE\fP and \fBUWG_HIDE_KEYS\fP spellings are
accepted as fallbacks; \fBWG_*\fP takes precedence.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/umbrawg/*.conf
Configuration files. The directory is readable only by root.
.TP
.I /var/run/umbrawg/*.sock
UAPI sockets of the userspace implementation.

.SH EXAMPLES
A simple client configuration in \fI/etc/umbrawg/uwg0.conf\fP:
.PP
.nf
[Interface]
Address = 10.200.100.8/24
DNS = 10.200.100.1
PrivateKey = oK56DE9Ue9zK76rAc8pBl6opph+1v36lm7cXXsQKrQM=

[Peer]
PublicKey = GtL7fZc/bLnqZldpVofMCD6hDjrK28SsdLxevJ+qtKU=
AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0
Endpoint = demo.example.net:51820
.fi
.PP
Bring it up and down with:
.PP
.nf
uwg-quick up uwg0
uwg-quick down uwg0
.fi

.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success and after printing help. A usage error (unknown subcommand,
missing or unexpected argument) or a runtime failure returns 1; diagnostics
and usage text are written to standard error.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uwg (8),
.BR ip (8),
.BR resolvconf (8),
.BR systemd (1)
